#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72513cd1-ed0c-5758-9f86-3910518bf8b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367011} {:answer/id #uuid "bc35329c-cdb3-52a4-83d0-badeab013971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u+v)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367012} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8bee01-6009-5cc9-b4da-b11c79f9139e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367013} {:answer/id #uuid "c315d17a-8d08-5f46-a406-dbc6dbf56435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({u}^{2}+{v}^{2})^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367014} {:answer/id #uuid "b041447f-cfcb-557f-8820-7eaa624d4c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u-v)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b8bee01-6009-5cc9-b4da-b11c79f9139e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367013} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e84f03c-45f8-5453-9d86-1686a4f61bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8eee31e-0f84-552f-8351-0fc856c749dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-104151" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{3} & =0 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f686e8d-5080-529c-b581-221892921cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined by an Implicit Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c184f9-1c9c-5efb-ae68-bd422886515d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-u-v}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367018} {:answer/id #uuid "034a13a3-e781-5842-8bf8-06461af74f75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-u-v}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367019} {:answer/id #uuid "23100d53-d663-5266-82f4-f67d51776d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-u-v}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367020} {:answer/id #uuid "464c331c-2bd2-59f4-b698-2b90c86bc867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-u-v}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367021} {:answer/id #uuid "893eca1f-0da5-54e8-9c5a-8280aab69731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+u-v}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "464c331c-2bd2-59f4-b698-2b90c86bc867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-u-v}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367021} :answer-field/id #uuid "6285d276-1886-507a-a7a6-cfbc81014574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "137fac61-848f-5605-9990-3509ba74c2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104179" :question/problem "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n$\\frac{x+y}{6}+\\frac{z-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6}+\\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{x+y}{6} \\\\ \\frac{z-1}{2} & =\\frac{1-x-y}{6} \\\\ z & =\\frac{4-x-y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\frac{4-x-y}{3}⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\frac{4-u-v}{3}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\frac{4-u-v}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "097a87b7-38e3-5002-b460-2edccb9ce85c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea7900ef-3018-5ff9-878c-a6bd05b1671b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367025} {:answer/id #uuid "d2141598-0071-5269-b9f5-ad5acf6bad89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367026} {:answer/id #uuid "f57b53e4-175d-5385-9a33-73a2ae525c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367027} {:answer/id #uuid "581f1dde-31e5-5675-ba28-901f6fa21f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367028} {:answer/id #uuid "881e9f53-642e-5201-bfd8-85dc24674352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581f1dde-31e5-5675-ba28-901f6fa21f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367028} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68f42b7-96fb-5fa3-a618-dbefcae9031f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d88762f9-7faa-5003-8c13-d18404076d4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104290" :question/problem "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$4{r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0$\nfor $z\\ge 0.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4{r}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{4{r}^{2}} \\\\ z & =2r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2r,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862df4d6-bcbb-53c9-94e3-a7f8b7f06d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367032} {:answer/id #uuid "c7567391-d9d3-51c6-9570-dcec53c18ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367033} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5de037-707f-595f-b502-aaac90e582b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367034} {:answer/id #uuid "1047f734-e4ef-5ef8-9ff6-2f9e3712abc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367035} {:answer/id #uuid "144b866d-f52d-5c1c-8a80-0c69d699910c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7567391-d9d3-51c6-9570-dcec53c18ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367033} :answer-field/id #uuid "92c2657d-bd5d-577d-93ea-9ecb446ac1e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa8239e-29e5-5977-9d1a-1751cf1acc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-104368" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6352a6-6505-517a-9a70-7c5701eda938" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface Defined in Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5fa5fac-c7d6-5164-bc27-c14d13354f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367039} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a4f705-6671-579c-a5d2-51c113a001f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367040} {:answer/id #uuid "df350d58-c4d8-585d-b4ce-dece66b8b1df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367041} {:answer/id #uuid "3ade17df-2b42-5f0a-a452-b6b26266325a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367042} {:answer/id #uuid "2181fc1a-93b5-5a4f-b537-5e1257f2bbb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a4f705-6671-579c-a5d2-51c113a001f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367040} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda4e1ea-8286-5816-9a57-f12f29bb748a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b3e84b4d-aff6-5599-897e-3da92a2fc9f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104378" :question/problem "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{ _____ },\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$"}]}
